</div>`;

<docs-article
  title="Installation"
  description="Install components as source you own, or import a themeable package — then wire up the CSS either path needs."
  href="/docs/installation"
  toc=TOC
>
  <p>
    marko-ui ships components two different ways. Pick the one that matches what you want to own:
  </p>

  <h2 id="choosing">Choosing a distribution</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>copy</strong> (<code>marko-ui add</code>) writes flat generated component source
    straight into your project — Tailwind utility classes baked in, no runtime dependency on
    marko-ui. This is the shadcn model: once it's in your repo, the code is yours to edit however
    you like.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>import</strong> installs <code>@marko-ui/shadcn</code>, a single npm package that ships
    the same components as <code>mu-*</code> hook-class components plus all 8 visual
    style layers. Nothing is copied into your project; you theme, override, or switch between
    styles entirely from your own stylesheet. Pick this when you expect to swap styles at runtime
    or theme centrally without maintaining a fork of the component source.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Note:</strong> neither <code>@marko-ui/shadcn</code> nor the <code>marko-ui</code> CLI is
    published to npm yet, so nothing below is installable today via <code>bunx</code>. The steps
    document the real, current CLI behavior — both distribution paths will work exactly as shown
    once the packages ship.
  </p>

  <h2 id="create-project">Create a project</h2>

  <p>Start from a Marko 6 app with Tailwind v4 already wired up. A fresh <code>@marko/run</code> app works:</p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=CREATE_APP/>

  <p>
    Any project with a Tailwind v4 stylesheet works — see
    <a href="/docs/components-json">components.json</a> for how the CLI detects your CSS file and
    path aliases.
  </p>

  <h2 id="init">marko-ui init</h2>

  <p>
    Run <code>marko-ui init</code> inside your project. With no flags it prompts interactively for
    base color, distribution, and visual style:
  </p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=INIT_INTERACTIVE/>

  <p>
    Or set everything non-interactively with <code>--distribution</code> and
    <code>--visual-style</code>:
  </p>

  <code-block language="bash" filename="copy distribution" code=INIT_COPY/>
  <code-block language="bash" filename="import distribution" code=INIT_IMPORT/>

  <p>
    <code>--distribution</code> accepts <code>copy</code> (the default) or <code>import</code>.
    <code>--visual-style</code> accepts <code>rhea</code>, <code>nova</code>, <code>vega</code>
    (the default), <code>lyra</code>, <code>maia</code>, <code>mira</code>, <code>luma</code>, or
    <code>sera</code> — see <a href="/docs/theming">Theming</a> for what each one changes. Both
    flags are recorded in <code>components.json</code> so later <code>add</code> and
    <code>eject</code> calls know which mode and style you're on.
  </p>

  <p>On <code>copy</code>, <code>init</code> writes <code>components.json</code>, then fetches and writes the theme matching your base color plus any components you named on the command line, as flat generated source under your components alias directory.</p>

  <p>On <code>import</code>, <code>init</code> writes <code>components.json</code>, adds <code>@marko-ui/shadcn</code> as a dependency, and scaffolds the CSS entry described below. No component files are written — there's nothing to copy.</p>

  <h2 id="add-components">Adding components</h2>

  <p>On the <code>copy</code> distribution, install any component by name:</p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=ADD_COMMAND/>

  <p>Several at once:</p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=ADD_MANY/>

  <p>
    The CLI resolves each item's registry dependencies, installs the <code>@zag-js/*</code>
    packages that component needs, and writes the <code>.marko</code> files under your components
    directory. Then import the tag by path and use it:
  </p>

  <code-block language="marko" code=USE_BUTTON_COPY/>

  <p>
    On the <code>import</code> distribution there is no <code>add</code> step — every component is
    already available from <code>@marko-ui/shadcn</code>:
  </p>

  <code-block language="marko" code=USE_BUTTON_IMPORT/>

  <p>
    Every component's page in the <a href="/docs/components">Components</a> section shows the
    exact install command and a live demo.
  </p>

  <h2 id="import-css">The import CSS entry</h2>

  <p>
    <code>marko-ui init --distribution import</code> writes this block into your Tailwind
    stylesheet automatically. If you're wiring it up by hand, or checking what got generated, it
    has to look exactly like this:
  </p>

  <code-block language="css" code=IMPORT_CSS_ENTRY/>

  <p>
    Two parts of this are easy to get wrong, and both fail silently — everything still renders, so
    the break only shows up later as "my override doesn't do anything."
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong><code>layer(components)</code> is required, not stylistic.</strong> Imported raw, a
    style layer's rules land outside any cascade layer, and unlayered CSS beats every layered
    declaration unconditionally — including Tailwind's own <code>@layer utilities</code>. Writing
    <code>class="rounded-none"</code> on a marko-ui <code>Button</code> silently loses to the style
    layer no matter how specific your selector is, if the layer was imported without
    <code>layer(components)</code>:
  </p>

  <code-block language="css" filename="don't do this" code=IMPORT_CSS_BROKEN/>

  <p>
    <strong>The <code>@custom-variant</code> line must be present.</strong> Components rely on
    Tailwind variants like <code>data-open:</code> and <code>data-state-*:</code>, which only exist
    because <code>globals.css</code> in the copy path defines them via <code>@custom-variant</code>.
    The import path doesn't get that for free — without the line above in your own build, those
    variant classes parse fine and compile to nothing. No error, no warning, just dead styling.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you picked a base color other than neutral at <code>init</code> time, the generated import
    points at <code>globals-&lt;color&gt;.css</code> instead of <code>globals.css</code> (for
    example <code>globals-zinc.css</code>), and the style layer and custom variant both use your
    chosen <code>--visual-style</code> in place of <code>vega</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="eject">marko-ui eject</h2>

  <p>
    <code>eject</code> switches a project from <code>import</code> to <code>copy</code>. It is not
    an escape hatch bolted on after the fact — under dual distribution it's a first-class,
    supported transition for when you outgrow "theme from CSS" and want to edit component source
    directly.
  </p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=EJECT_COMMAND/>

  <p>
    It reads which components are actually in use from <code>@marko-ui/shadcn</code>'s installed
    <code>ui/</code> tree, cross-checks that list against the registry, then fetches and writes
    local source for every one of them — the exact same fetch-and-write path <code>add</code> uses,
    so the result is indistinguishable from having run <code>add</code> for each component by hand.
    It then flips <code>components.json</code>'s <code>distribution</code> field to
    <code>copy</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>eject</code> does not remove the <code>@marko-ui/shadcn</code> npm dependency or the CSS
    block <code>init --distribution import</code> wrote — the command prints both as manual
    follow-up steps, since your project keeps rendering correctly with the old CSS in place until
    you remove it and reinstall the copy-path theme (<code>marko-ui add style</code>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="mu-hooks">mu-* styling hooks</h2>

  <p>
    Every marko-ui component carries semantic <code>mu-*</code> classes in its markup —
    <code>mu-button</code>, <code>mu-accordion-trigger</code>, and so on — regardless of which
    distribution you're on. On <code>copy</code> these are inert decoration; the generated Tailwind
    utility classes sitting alongside them do the actual styling, and editing that file is how you
    change anything.
  </p>

  <p>
    On <code>import</code> they're load-bearing: <code>mu-*</code> is the public styling API a
    consumer's own stylesheet targets. Scope an override to one visual style by combining it with
    the <code>style-&lt;name&gt;</code> class your CSS entry activates:
  </p>

  <code-block language="css" code=MU_OVERRIDE_CSS/>

  <p>
    Switching styles at runtime — no rebuild, no reinstall — is just toggling which
    <code>style-&lt;name&gt;</code> class is present on an ancestor element. All 8
    layers ship in the package at once, so this works the moment you import more than one of them:
  </p>

  <code-block language="marko" code=MU_SWITCH_CLASS/>

  <h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h2>

  <h3>An override or a data-* variant class does nothing</h3>
  <p>
    On the import distribution this is almost always the CSS entry: either the style layer was
    imported without <code>layer(components)</code>, or the matching
    <code>@custom-variant style-&lt;name&gt;</code> line is missing. Both fail silently — see
    <a href="#import-css">the import CSS entry</a> above.
  </p>

  <h3>Components render unstyled</h3>
  <p>
    On the copy distribution, this is almost always a missing Tailwind <code>@source</code>
    directive for your component directory. Tailwind v4 has to be told where your
    <code>.marko</code> files live, and needs a second directive if components were installed
    outside the directory the first one covers.
  </p>

  <h3>"Cannot find module" for a @zag-js package</h3>
  <p>
    <code>marko-ui add</code> installs a component's declared dependencies automatically. If you
    copied files manually instead, install them yourself — the list is in the component's registry
    JSON under <code>dependencies</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>Serialization errors on interaction</h3>
  <p>
    A Zag service or API object escaped into reactive state. This is a component-authoring mistake
    rather than an installation problem — see <a href="/docs/creating-components">Creating
    Components</a> for the rule and the fix.
  </p>
</docs-article>
